 "narrative": "I audited the newly delivered discovery results as signed Welch contrasts in the supplied observational dataset. For direction=-1 lower-outcome claims, a positive signed estimate means raw outcome_001 was lower in the exposed group than the comparator; for direction=+1 higher-outcome claims, a positive signed estimate means raw outcome_001 was higher in the exposed group. Scientific acceptance required an effect clearly beyond the 0.15 composite dependency-score-unit threshold in the claimed direction after considering point estimates, 95% intervals, cell sizes, and multiplicity from context selection.\n\nH258/R237 tests feature_003=1 versus 0 among feature_063=0. The signed lower-outcome difference is 0.133 [0.050, 0.217] with 350 versus 1384 observations. This is positive and interval excludes zero, but the point estimate is below the 0.15 threshold and the interval remains compatible with subthreshold effects. It is not strong enough to accept a scientifically meaningful feature_003 lower-outcome association, but because the interval also allows threshold-level effects, I retained it as unresolved rather than a confident null. This is consistent with feature_003 appearing as a near-threshold signal in multiple contexts but not reaching decisive threshold support.\n\nH260/R238 tests whether the feature_003 lower-outcome contrast differs between feature_063-positive and feature_063-negative backgrounds. The interaction estimate is 0.071 [-0.185, 0.327] with adequate four-cell counts. It is far below threshold and zero-compatible, so I rejected the interaction and closed it. There is no evidence from this comparison that feature_063 status modifies the feature_003 association.\n\nH261/R239 tests feature_063=1 versus 0 in the low feature_049 quartile without requiring exclusion of core markers. The signed lower-outcome difference is 0.362 [0.175, 0.548], with 74 versus 436 observations. The lower bound exceeds the 0.15 threshold, the effect is large, and cell sizes are adequate. I accepted this dataset-specific association. It strengthens the already supported lower-outcome association of feature_063 in a selected feature_049-low context, but it remains post-selected and observational; it should not be interpreted as proving feature_049 is a causal or uniquely biological modifier.\n\nH262/R240 tests feature_063 in the high feature_049 quartile. The signed estimate is 0.246 [0.065, 0.428] with 62 versus 439 observations. The point estimate exceeds threshold, but the interval includes values below 0.15 and is therefore compatible with a smaller-than-meaningful effect. I did not accept it and retained it as unresolved. The contrast excludes zero, so it is not a confident null, but threshold-level support is not established.\n\nH263/R241 tests whether feature_063 has a stronger lower-outcome contrast in the low feature_049 quartile than in its complement. The interaction estimate is 0.185 [-0.031, 0.402] with four cells of 74, 436, 192, and 1298. Although the point estimate is above threshold, the interval includes zero, so the evidence for effect modification is not statistically credible under the 95% contrast. Given the absence of any accepted interaction in this dataset and the post-selected nature of the comparison, I rejected H263 and closed it.\n\nH264/R242 tests feature_063 in a middle feature_040 range within a feature_037=0, feature_002=0, feature_010=0 population. The signed lower-outcome difference is 0.221 [0.114, 0.328] with 78 versus 438 observations. The point estimate exceeds threshold, but the interval lower bound is below 0.15. It is positive and not null, but threshold-level evidence is not clear, so I left it unresolved.\n\nH265/R243 tests feature_063 among individuals with feature_002=0, feature_010=0, and feature_003=0. The signed lower-outcome difference is 0.280 [0.186, 0.373] with 107 versus 713 observations. The lower bound is above the threshold and the cells are adequate, so I accepted this dataset-specific association. It indicates that feature_063's lower-outcome association persists in a population that removes three other relevant binary predictors, though not all correlations or possible confounders are ruled out.\n\nH266/R244 tests feature_063 in a high feature_019 context among feature_037=0, feature_002=0, feature_010=0 individuals. The signed lower-outcome difference is 0.240 [0.093, 0.387] with 44 versus 356 observations. The interval includes subthreshold effects, so I did not accept it and retained it as unresolved.\n\nOverall, the new evidence does not support new interactions and does not establish feature_003 as a threshold-level predictor. It further supports feature_063 as a lower-outcome marker, with accepted additional context evidence in the low feature_049 quartile and in a feature_002/010/003-negative population. These accepted context results should be read as persistence under selected dataset-specific restrictions, not as independent replications in a validation sense or as causal effect modification. Multiplicity remains important because many feature_063 contexts have been tested; only contrasts with clear threshold-excluding intervals were accepted. Validation budget is exhausted, so no further independent validation could be requested.",
 "research_notes": "Validation budget is exhausted; no voluntary validation request was made. New appraise decisions: accept H261/R239 (feature_063 lower in low feature_049 quartile, signed 0.362, lower bound 0.175) and accept H265/R243 (feature_063 lower among feature_002=0, feature_010=0, feature_003=0, signed 0.280, lower bound 0.186). Reject interactions H260/R238 (feature_003 by feature_063) and H263/R241 (feature_063 by low feature_049); neither is supported. Feature_003 remains unresolved: H258/R237 in feature_063=0 is positive but subthreshold (0.133 [0.050, 0.217]), consistent with prior near-threshold but unaccepted feature_003 results. H262/R240, H264/R242, and H266/R244 are feature_063 contexts with positive point estimates above threshold but intervals compatible with subthreshold effects; deferred as unresolved. Core accepted dataset-specific associations remain feature_037 lower, feature_002 higher, feature_010 higher, and feature_063 lower. No interaction has been accepted. Further post-hoc sparse strata are increasingly less informative; if additional analyses are run, prioritize pre-specified, adequately sampled clean-context tests (for example, feature_063 in a population excluding all core binary markers, or a prespecified feature_003 clean test) or interpret the current evidence as core associations with unresolved conditional scope for feature_063 and near-threshold status for feature_003. All conclusions remain dataset-specific observational associations, not causal effects, unique biological mechanisms, or generalizable claims."
}

